  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 312 PM EDT Tue Sep 1 2026\n\nAbove-average temperatures and breezy conditions are expected to \nremain over the area through this afternoon. In the western CWA, RH \nvalues in the low 30s indicate the potential for borderline fire \nweather conditions to persist through this time period. Tonight, the \nlow pressure center currently over Ontario will pass to the north as \nit moves eastward toward Quebec. \n\nThis progression of the low is expected to return upper-level \nmoisture over the area tonight, increasing PWATs to 1.00-1.25 inches \nby Wednesday morning. Chances for rain begin Wednesday around 13z, \nwith the best chances (up to 45%) occuring between 15z and 16z in \nthe southern portion of the CWA. Precipitation chances are expected \nto decrease and skies are expected to partially clear towards the \nearly afternoon due to the return of drier upper-level air over the \narea as the Canadian low continues its progression eastward. \n\nMuch of the remainder of the week will be dominated by upper level \nridging over the central CONUS and broad troughing over eastern \nCanada, leaving the Upper Great Lakes in a fairly benign northwest \nflow pattern between the two features. The influence of the ridge \nwill be enough to keep temperatures above seasonal norms across the \nthe UP through the period, with daytime highs staying in the mid to \nupper 70s and overnight lows generally in the mid to upper 50s \nthrough the weekend. Do not see any significant rainfall in the \nforecast beyond Wednesday, but a few disturbances embedded in the \nflow will at least keep some periodic shower chances around from \nThursday into the weekend.",
